In a society governed by geometry, the first thing to examine is the **form** of the act itself. A solitary weekend hobby that harms no one and costs no one can only be imagined as a **closed, bounded figure**—a circle or ellipse that is complete in itself, its boundary clearly separating it from surrounding shapes. Because it does not intersect, overlap, or intrude upon any neighbor’s figure, there is no geometric relationship (no shared edge, no crossing line, no distortion of tessellation) from which a moral obligation or violation could derive. The neighbors’ concern, by contrast, projects outward like angular rays or penetrating vertices aimed at this enclosed space; geometrically, theirs is the active imposition, not his.

That said, a community does possess some geometric weight if it understands itself as a larger pattern requiring a degree of regularity or harmony among its constituent shapes. Yet without actual spatial impact—without injury or loss erasing a boundary—the claim of “impropriety” reduces to an aesthetic reaction to an unfamiliar curve inside an otherwise rectilinear neighborhood. It is the difference between an objective geometric fact (non-intersection) and a subjective preference for uniform angles. The deepest moral weight therefore rests on respecting the integrity of the closed figure and distinguishing genuine formal violation from mere discomfort.

